-- M4 (spec §10): the `review:` block as a COMPLIANCE-CHECKLIST view.
--
-- The rule is short-circuiting on purpose. With `the return was filed` FALSE,
-- the other three inputs decide nothing and a backchaining interview never
-- asks them — which is the property the backend sells, and also the property
-- that makes a plain answer summary misleading: a summary that lists only the
-- answers given says nothing about the three questions the law never had to
-- put. A compliance checklist must show all four rows, and must distinguish
-- "answered NO" from "never asked".
--
-- WHAT `skip undefined: False` ACTUALLY DOES, measured. Spec §10 called for
-- a `review:` block "with `skip undefined: False`". That flag does not
-- produce a passive checklist: it makes the review block FORCE-ASK every
-- undefined variable it lists (parse.py:5876-5904 at 1b6678384 — with the
-- flag the row's eval is no longer wrapped in try/except). The default
-- (absent) is the opposite failure: an undefined row is SILENTLY DROPPED into
-- a debug log line. Neither is a checklist; one interrogates the user about
-- questions the law never reached, the other hides them.
--
-- The recipe that does work, probed against 1.10.7: `note:` rows carrying
-- `showifdef('<var>', '<not asked>')`. A note row has no `saveas` to evaluate,
-- so it always renders, giving one row per L4 input including the never-asked
-- ones. That is the whole of what M4 emits.
--
-- NOT `Edit:` rows. This paragraph used to end "pair each with an `Edit:` row
-- gated on `show if: defined('<var>')` so the edit affordance appears only
-- where there is an answer to edit". That probe result is real, but the GREEN
-- phase then declined to emit `Edit:` rows at all (spec §10, "What M4 did NOT
-- do"): the checklist is a VIEW, and the answer-changing path it exists to make
-- ordinary is tested through `undefine()` + re-ask, which is what §8.4's repair
-- was measured against. Emitting edit affordances is a UI decision with its own
-- probes owing. Every one of the thirteen committed goldens has zero `Edit:`
-- rows; the sentence was retracted here on 2026-08-17 so this file, which is
-- what a reader opens to understand the review block, stops describing a shape
-- the emitter does not produce.

DECLARE Filing
    HAS
      @desc Whether the annual return was filed at all
      `the return was filed`   IS A BOOLEAN
      @desc Whether the annual return was filed by the statutory deadline
      `the return was on time` IS A BOOLEAN
      @desc Whether the filing fee was paid
      `the fee was paid`       IS A BOOLEAN
      @desc Whether the registrar granted a waiver of the filing fee
      `a waiver was granted`   IS A BOOLEAN

@export Whether the annual filing obligation has been discharged
GIVEN f IS A Filing @desc The annual filing under review
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the filing obligation is discharged` f IF
        f's `the return was filed`
    AND f's `the return was on time`
    AND (f's `the fee was paid` OR f's `a waiver was granted`)

-- Everything in order.
#EVAL `the filing obligation is discharged` OF
        Filing WITH
            `the return was filed`   IS TRUE
            `the return was on time` IS TRUE
            `the fee was paid`       IS TRUE
            `a waiver was granted`   IS FALSE
-- TRUE

-- Fee unpaid but waived.
#EVAL `the filing obligation is discharged` OF
        Filing WITH
            `the return was filed`   IS TRUE
            `the return was on time` IS TRUE
            `the fee was paid`       IS FALSE
            `a waiver was granted`   IS TRUE
-- TRUE

-- NOTHING WAS FILED. The remaining three inputs are short-circuited away and
-- must never be asked — and must still appear on the checklist, marked as
-- never asked. This is the case the review block exists for.
#EVAL `the filing obligation is discharged` OF
        Filing WITH
            `the return was filed`   IS FALSE
            `the return was on time` IS FALSE
            `the fee was paid`       IS FALSE
            `a waiver was granted`   IS FALSE
-- FALSE
